Give the add-on the four things upstream never generated: core options, an
input map, a controller topology, and an icon.

resources/settings.xml is the fatal one. Without it Kodi answers every
RETRO_ENVIRONMENT_GET_VARIABLE with "not found", so the core reads every option
back as 0 or NULL. retro_get_system_av_info then computes its geometry as
640x448 times pcsx2_upscale_multiplier -- which is 0 -- and the client reports a
maximum frame size of 0x0, so RetroPlayer refuses to open the hardware
rendering stream. pcsx2_bios reads back empty too and the load fails with
"Could not find any valid PS2 BIOS File".

It is generated by negotiating core options v2 rather than the v0
SET_VARIABLES fallback, so the categories, the help text and a label for every
value survive -- "Standard (4:3)" instead of "0". v0 also truncates some lists:
it offers six upscale multipliers where the core declares ten. Settings stay
type="string" because game.libretro hands the chosen value straight to the
core; Kodi's old "lvalues" attribute would make the setting an int whose value
is the list index, sending 0 where the core expects 1.

Upstream never generated any of it because kodi-game-scripting harvests options
by calling only retro_set_environment, while LRPS2 registers its options from
retro_init -- and returns early from there unless it finds a BIOS, because the
pcsx2_bios and memory-card option lists are built from what is on disk.

Note the pcsx2_bios values are therefore whatever BIOS images were present when
the list was harvested: SCPH30004R.bin (Europe v01.60) and scph39001.bin. A box
with differently named BIOS files needs this list regenerated.

buttonmap.xml declares the pad as RETRO_DEVICE_JOYPAD, matching the
retro_controller_description LRPS2 actually registers, rather than
RETRO_DEVICE_ANALOG as the physical DualShock would suggest. Getting that wrong
hands retro_set_controller_port_device() a device the core does not know and no
input reaches the game, silently -- the same mistake that cost the Dreamcast pad
its input in game.libretro.flycast. The analog sticks still map; the core reads
them through RETRO_DEVICE_ANALOG regardless of the declared type.

topology.xml declares two ports, which is what the core's retro_controller_info
array holds. The PS2 reaches eight pads through a multitap but LRPS2 does not
expose them.

The name gains "OpenGL ES" because the libretro-buildbot PCSX2 add-on reports
the identical name "Sony - PlayStation 2 (LRPS2)", and with no icon on this one
the two were indistinguishable in the game client picker.

The buttonmap declares version="2" on the root tag. game.libretro rejects a
buttonmap with no version attribute outright -- "Buttonmap version required,
expected version 2 (min=2)" -- and then every feature reports "does not
resolve, the core will never see it", so the pad is dead while the controller
profiles still appear in the port dialog with nothing to map. The v2 body
format is identical to v1; the attribute is the whole difference.

